<h3 id="structure-field-access-cost-estimate">Structure field access cost estimate</h3>

<ul>
 <li>SBCL: \(c_s \approx 3.2\times 10^{-10}\)s.</li>
 <li>LispWorks: \(c_s \approx 3.1\times 10^{-10}\)s.</li></ul>

<p>Note that these are both almost certainly a single cycle up to rounding.</p>

<h3 id="standard-instance-subclass-slot-access-cost-estimate"><code>standard-instance</code> subclass slot access cost estimate</h3>

<ul>
 <li>SBCL: \(c_s \approx 1.2\times 10^{-8}\)s.</li>
 <li>LispWorks: \(c_s \approx 1.0\times 10^{-8}\)s.</li></ul>

<h3 id="ratios">Ratios</h3>

<p>The ratios between these two values for each implementation are then about 38 for SBCL and about 32 for LispWorks: this is how much slower CLOS slot access is than structure field access. In fact it is probably an underestimate of how much slower it is.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-obvious2026-05-25-measuring-slot-access-cost-in-common-lisp-footnote-1-definition2026-05-25-measuring-slot-access-cost-in-common-lisp-footnote-1-return1">What is obvious<sup><a href="https://www.tfeb.org/fragments/feeds/lisp.rss.xml#2026-05-25-measuring-slot-access-cost-in-common-lisp-footnote-1-definition" name="2026-05-25-measuring-slot-access-cost-in-common-lisp-footnote-1-return">1</a></sup></h2>

<p>CLOS slot access is really slow.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-less-obvious-but-almost-certainly-true2026-05-25-measuring-slot-access-cost-in-common-lisp-footnote-2-definition2026-05-25-measuring-slot-access-cost-in-common-lisp-footnote-2-return2">What is less obvious but almost certainly true<sup><a href="https://www.tfeb.org/fragments/feeds/lisp.rss.xml#2026-05-25-measuring-slot-access-cost-in-common-lisp-footnote-2-definition" name="2026-05-25-measuring-slot-access-cost-in-common-lisp-footnote-2-return">2</a></sup></h2>

<p>This is not because multiple inheritance is inherently slow: it&rsquo;s because the design of CLOS, especially if you want to take the AMOP MOP seriously, implies crappy performance.</p>

<p>Can this be fixed? Yes, I think so, with well-defined tradeoffs. Will it be? Up to implementors. So, probably not, sadly.</p>

